+### §check-fails-open — the check aborts non-zero on any failing command (fails closed; reverses the fails-open rule)
+- Since: 2026-10-23
+- Decision: `set -euo pipefail`. Any failing command aborts the check with a non-zero exit. A run in which no command
+  fails still ends in `exit 0`, including when it warns about large staged files. The user (repo maintainer) overrode
+  the earlier fails-open decision on 2026-10-23; no further rationale was recorded.
+- Rejected: fails-open — `set -uo pipefail` without `-e`, every internal error ending in `exit 0`, the script only
+  warning (the rule recorded from 2026-09-11 until 2026-10-23).
+- Reason: reversed at the maintainer's direction, knowingly. Original rationale for the rejected rule, kept for history:
+  with `set -e`, a `wc` failure on a staged-then-deleted file aborted the check and blocked every commit in the
+  repository for a day (2026-09-10); the check was advisory by design. That risk is now accepted, so a failing command
+  in the check can block commits where it runs as a hook. The anchor keeps its old name so existing references still
+  resolve.
